All nodes can be gathered first, then ordered by their values. The sorted nodes are linked again with `child`, while every `next` link is removed. This keeps the same node objects instead of allocating a second list.

The input structure is changed in place. A null head returns immediately, and a one-node list also works without any special extra step.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public ListNode FlattenLinkedList(ListNode head)
    {
        if (head == null)
        {
            return null;
        }

        List<ListNode> nodes = new List<ListNode>();
        ListNode row = head;

        while (row != null)
        {
            ListNode current = row;
            while (current != null)
            {
                nodes.Add(current);
                current = current.child;
            }
            row = row.next;
        }

        nodes.Sort((a, b) => a.val.CompareTo(b.val));

        for (int i = 0; i < nodes.Count; i++)
        {
            nodes[i].next = null;
            nodes[i].child = i + 1 < nodes.Count ? nodes[i + 1] : null;
        }

        return nodes[0];
    }
}
```

The list of references is extra storage, but the actual linked-list nodes are reused. Sorting the references also avoids changing values inside any node.

Time complexity: `O(n log n)`. Space complexity: `O(n)` for the stored node references.

This version collects the nodes and inserts them one by one into a sorted child-only list. It avoids calling a library sort, though insertion can take a full walk for many nodes, so it is mainly a simple learner version.

The old `next` pointers are cleared as each node enters the result. The original nodes are reused, and an empty head is returned unchanged as `nil`.

```go
package main

/*
type ListNode struct {
	Val   int
	Next  *ListNode
	Child *ListNode
}
*/

func insertSorted(head *ListNode, node *ListNode) *ListNode {
	node.Next = nil
	node.Child = nil

	if head == nil || node.Val < head.Val {
		node.Child = head
		return node
	}

	current := head
	for current.Child != nil && current.Child.Val <= node.Val {
		current = current.Child
	}

	node.Child = current.Child
	current.Child = node
	return head
}

func flattenLinkedList(head *ListNode) *ListNode {
	if head == nil {
		return nil
	}

	nodes := make([]*ListNode, 0)
	row := head

	for row != nil {
		current := row
		for current != nil {
			nodes = append(nodes, current)
			current = current.Child
		}
		row = row.Next
	}

	var result *ListNode
	for _, node := range nodes {
		result = insertSorted(result, node)
	}

	return result
}
```

For an input with just one vertical chain, the nodes are inserted in order and no rearrangement is needed. Repeated values are also kept correctly because equal values are placed after existing equal values.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case. Space complexity: `O(n)` for the slice of node pointers.

The child chain of each top-level node is sorted. I flatten the lists on the right first, then merge that result with the current child chain. The merge works like merging two sorted linked lists.

This changes the original links in place. After flattening, every node is connected through `child`, and `next` is set to `nullptr`.

```cpp
/*
Definition of special linked list:
struct ListNode
{
    int val;
    ListNode *next;
    ListNode *child;
};
*/

class Solution {
    ListNode* mergeLists(ListNode* first, ListNode* second) {
        ListNode dummy(0);
        ListNode* tail = &dummy;

        while (first != nullptr && second != nullptr) {
            if (first->val <= second->val) {
                tail->child = first;
                first = first->child;
            } else {
                tail->child = second;
                second = second->child;
            }

            tail = tail->child;
            tail->next = nullptr;
        }

        tail->child = (first != nullptr) ? first : second;

        ListNode* current = dummy.child;
        while (current != nullptr) {
            current->next = nullptr;
            current = current->child;
        }

        return dummy.child;
    }

    ListNode* flattenFromRight(ListNode* head) {
        if (head == nullptr || head->next == nullptr) {
            return head;
        }

        ListNode* remaining = flattenFromRight(head->next);
        return mergeLists(head, remaining);
    }

public:
    ListNode* flattenLinkedList(ListNode* &head) {
        return flattenFromRight(head);
    }
};
```

For example, if the first chain starts with 1 and the already flattened right side starts with 2, the merge links 1 first and then continues with the smaller available child each time.

Time complexity: `O(n * r)` in the usual layout, where `r` is the number of top-level lists. Space complexity: `O(r)` from recursive calls.

Each vertical list is already sorted, so I put the first node of every list into a min-heap. After removing the smallest node, its child is added to the heap. This keeps the output sorted without sorting all values again.

The result uses `child` links only. The old `next` links are cleared while building the flattened list. An empty input is handled before creating the heap.

```java
import java.util.PriorityQueue;

class Solution {
    public ListNode flattenLinkedList(ListNode head) {
        if (head == null) {
            return null;
        }

        PriorityQueue<ListNode> heap = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> Integer.compare(a.val, b.val));
        ListNode row = head;
        while (row != null) {
            heap.offer(row);
            row = row.next;
        }

        ListNode result = null;
        ListNode tail = null;

        while (!heap.isEmpty()) {
            ListNode current = heap.poll();
            ListNode nextChild = current.child;

            current.next = null;
            current.child = null;

            if (result == null) {
                result = current;
            } else {
                tail.child = current;
            }
            tail = current;

            if (nextChild != null) {
                heap.offer(nextChild);
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

For `3 -> 7` and `2 -> 5`, the heap first returns 2, then 3, then 5, and finally 7.

Time complexity: `O(n log r)`, where `r` is the number of top-level lists. Space complexity: `O(r)` for the heap.

## Intuition
Each `next` is a new sorted column hanging on `child`. Do not dump every value into an array. Flatten everything to the right first so it becomes **one** sorted `child` list, then merge that with the current column (already sorted). Same zip as merge sort, two lists at a time.


## Approach
1. If `head` is null or `head->next` is null, this column is already done.
2. Recurse on `head->next` so the whole right side becomes one sorted list.
3. Merge this column with that list using `child` (pick the smaller head each time).
4. Set `next = NULL` on nodes you attach so the answer is a single layer.
5. When one list ends, hang the leftover: `temp->child = a ? a : b`.

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution {

public:
    ListNode* merge(ListNode* a, ListNode* b){

        ListNode* dummy = new ListNode(0);
        ListNode* temp = dummy;

        while(a && b){
            if(a->val <= b->val){
                temp->child = a;
                a = a->child;
            }
            else{
                temp->child = b;
                b = b->child;
            }
            temp = temp->child;        
            temp->next = NULL; // making only single layer
        }
        temp->child = a ? a : b; // if a is not NULL → use a otherise use b

        return dummy->child;
    }

    ListNode* flattenLinkedList(ListNode* &head) {

        if(!head || !head->next) return head;

        head->next = flattenLinkedList(head->next); // flatten right side
        head = merge(head, head->next);

        return head;
    }
};
```

## Complexity
Time Complexity: O(N · k)
N = total nodes, k = number of columns. Each merge walks lists you already built, like merging k sorted lists one after another.

Space Complexity: O(k)
