Time Complexity
O(N * k) where N is the total number of nodes in the multi-level structure and k is the number of nodes in the horizontal 'next' list. This is because each merge operation takes O(M + L) where M is the accumulated size of previously merged nodes and L is the size of the current list being merged; in the worst case, the sum of these merge operations results in a quadratic relationship between the number of lists and total nodes.
Space Complexity
O(1) because the merging is performed iteratively by rearranging the existing node pointers (child and next) without the use of recursion stacks or auxiliary data structures.
